// std/group/normal-subgroup.b4m — the theory of a NORMAL SUBGROUP, as a MODELABLE theory.
//
// `std/group/normal.b4m` develops normality the other way round: it proves the conjugation ⟺
// coset equivalence and then takes normality as a HYPOTHESIS on each theorem. That is right for
// the equivalence itself, but it means "N is a normal subgroup" has no reified form — every
// consumer re-states the four conditions in its own statement, and a consumer with a concrete N
// cannot say "this is a normal subgroup" once and be done.
//
// This file reifies it. Normality is an AXIOM here, alongside the three subgroup criteria, so
// the whole package is a theory a concrete subgroup MODELS — and in return the corpus below
// transfers onto its own predicate.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE FILE FROM normal.b4m. Two reasons, both about what a name can hold. First,
// `normal.b4m`'s theorems are stated with normality as an antecedent, and the same names here
// would be a different claim (holding ON a normal subgroup, unconditionally) — a file cannot
// carry both. Second, and more practically: `normal.b4m` imports `coset.b4m` for the coset
// characterisation, which this theory does not need. A theory meant to be modeled should ask
// for as little as possible, because every import is something a consumer's model must keep
// consistent.
//
// WHAT A CONSUMER OWES, and the one thing the engine does not yet check. A model of this theory
// must map the sort, the three group operations, and `inSubgroup` — and discharge four axioms.
// If a consumer FORGETS to overload `inSubgroup` (mapping only the sort and operations), the
// transfer proceeds against this file's own opaque predicate rather than the consumer's, and
// the resulting theorem is about the wrong subgroup. Nothing currently detects that. It is a
// known soundness hole, accepted deliberately; the guarantees are future work. Until then a
// consumer's model block is load-bearing and should be read as carefully as a proof.

import group <<< "std/group.b4m"
import subgroup <<< "std/subgroup.b4m"

// The ambient group, ALIASED — one origin, as in subgroup.b4m.
sort Grp = group.Grp
const E = group.E
func op = group.op
func inverse = group.inverse

fact grpAssoc = group.opAssoc
fact grpIdentityLeft = group.opIdentityLeft
fact grpIdentityRight = group.opIdentityRight
fact grpInverseLeft = group.opInverseLeft
fact grpInverseRight = group.opInverseRight
fact invInvolution = group.invInvolution
fact invProduct = group.invProduct

// The subgroup criteria, ALIASED from `subgroup.b4m` rather than re-declared: a normal
// subgroup IS a subgroup, so its membership predicate and three criteria are that theory's.
// A consumer modeling THIS theory therefore satisfies the subgroup theory too, and
// `subgroup.b4m`'s corpus is available alongside this one.
pred inSubgroup = subgroup.inSubgroup
fact subgroupHasIdentity = subgroup.subgroupHasIdentity
fact subgroupClosedOp = subgroup.subgroupClosedOp
fact subgroupClosedInverse = subgroup.subgroupClosedInverse

// NORMALITY, as the conjugation condition. This is the one axiom the file adds, and it is the
// form every later proof uses — `normal.b4m` proves it equivalent to the coset condition
// (`conjugationLandsInTheSubgroupIffCosetsCoincide`), so stating it this way loses nothing.
axiom subgroupIsNormal: forall g: Grp; forall n: Grp;
  inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(g, n), inverse(g)))

// WHY THIS THEORY DECLARES NO PREDICATED SORT. `subgroup.b4m` has `Sub = Grp where
// inSubgroup` and states its corpus over it, which reads well — but normality is the one
// property whose statement quantifies over the AMBIENT group as well as the subgroup
// (`∀g ∈ G, ∀n ∈ N`). A predicated sort over `Grp` is indistinguishable from `Grp` to the
// transfer: mapping it relativizes EVERY binder over the carrier, so the conjugator `g`
// acquires `inSubgroup(g)` and the transferred theorem says only that N is closed under
// conjugation BY ITS OWN ELEMENTS — a strictly weaker claim, and not normality.
//
// Measured: with `Nml = Grp where inSubgroup` mapped onto A_n, the transfer demanded
// `inAlternating(g)` for the conjugator; without the mapping the derived sort does not remap
// at all ("expected sort 'Fn', got 'Grp'"). So the corpus below takes membership as an
// explicit PREMISE and quantifies only over `Grp`. Less pretty, and correct.

// -- the corpus ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// CONJUGATION BY AN ARBITRARY AMBIENT ELEMENT STAYS INSIDE. The axiom itself, re-exported so
// a consumer gets it under a name that says what it means.
theorem conjugationStaysInside: forall g: Grp; forall n: Grp;
  inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(g, n), inverse(g)))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall g: Grp; forall n: Grp;
      inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(g, n), inverse(g)))
    [by cite subgroupIsNormal]
qed

// CONJUGATION BY AN INVERSE STAYS INSIDE TOO — Judson's observation that g⁻¹ works wherever g
// does, which is his (2)⇒(3). The axiom at `inverse(g)` plus the involution law rewriting
// `inverse(inverse(g))` back to `g`.
theorem conjugationByAnInverseStaysInside: forall g: Grp; forall n: Grp;
  inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), g))
proof
  @generalize-g |
    fix g: Grp {
      @generalize-n |
        fix n: Grp {
          @given-n-in-the-subgroup |
            assume inSubgroup(n) {
              @n-is-a-member |
                inSubgroup(n)
                [by hypothesis given-n-in-the-subgroup]
              @conjugate-by-the-inverse |
                inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), inverse(inverse(g))))
                [using specialize subgroupIsNormal(inverse(g), n) n-is-a-member]
              @the-inverse-is-involutive |
                inverse(inverse(g)) = g
                [using specialize invInvolution(g)]
              @conclusion-conjugate-is-inside |
                inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), g))
                [by rewrite the-inverse-is-involutive conjugate-by-the-inverse]
            }
          @conclusion-at-n |
            inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), g))
            [by discharge given-n-in-the-subgroup]
        }
      @discharge-n |
        forall n: Grp; inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), g))
        [by generalize generalize-n]
    }
  @conclusion |
    forall g: Grp; forall n: Grp;
      inSubgroup(n) -> inSubgroup(op(op(inverse(g), n), g))
    [by generalize generalize-g]
qed
